{
  "concept": "Rotating the coordinate axes by an angle θ can align a tilted ellipse with the new axes, changing the same conic from an x,y equation with an xy term into a simpler x′,y′ equation with no cross-product term.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "thin black horizontal x-axis with double-headed arrow, labeled x at the positive end",
    "thin black vertical y-axis with double-headed arrow, labeled y at the positive end",
    "shared origin where both coordinate systems intersect",
    "orange rotated x′-axis with double-headed arrow running from lower left to upper right",
    "orange rotated y′-axis with double-headed arrow perpendicular to x′, running from lower right to upper left",
    "label x′ near the positive end of the rotated x′-axis",
    "label y′ near the positive end of the rotated y′-axis",
    "dark blue ellipse centered at the origin",
    "ellipse major axis aligned with the orange x′-axis",
    "ellipse minor axis aligned with the orange y′-axis",
    "small curved angle arc from the positive x-axis to the positive x′-axis",
    "label θ beside the angle arc",
    "original-coordinate equation display, showing an x,y conic with an xy term highlighted",
    "rotated-coordinate equation display, showing an x′,y′ conic with no x′y′ term highlighted",
    "clickable point on the ellipse",
    "projection segments from the selected point to the black x and y axes",
    "projection segments from the selected point to the orange x′ and y′ axes",
    "coordinate readout for the selected point in the original frame, labeled (x, y)",
    "coordinate readout for the same selected point in the rotated frame, labeled (x′, y′)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thetaRotation",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Rotation angle θ",
      "range": [0, 90, 1],
      "default": 45,
      "teaches": "The ellipse appears misaligned in the fixed x,y frame but becomes axis-aligned when the rotated x′,y′ frame turns to match its principal directions"
    },
    {
      "id": "equationView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Equation form",
      "options": ["Original x,y equation", "Rotated x′,y′ equation"],
      "default": "Original x,y equation",
      "teaches": "The xy term belongs to the original tilted view of the conic, while the rotated frame removes that cross term by measuring along the ellipse's own axes"
    },
    {
      "id": "pointComparison",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "A point on the ellipse is unchanged geometrically, but its coordinate values depend on which axis system is used to measure it"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "See the tilted conic in the original frame",
      "narration": "The black x and y axes stay fixed while the ellipse is tilted across them. In this original coordinate system, the displayed equation includes an xy term because the conic is not aligned with the axes.",
      "state": {
        "thetaRotation": 45,
        "equationView": "Original x,y equation"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Rotate the coordinate frame",
      "narration": "Drag θ and watch the orange x′ and y′ axes turn with the ellipse while the black axes remain fixed. The key alignment occurs when x′ follows the ellipse's long axis and y′ follows its short axis.",
      "state": {
        "thetaRotation": 45,
        "equationView": "Original x,y equation"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare equation forms",
      "narration": "Switch to the rotated equation: the active highlight moves from the xy term to the squared x′ and y′ terms. In the rotated frame, the ellipse behaves like a standard axis-aligned ellipse.",
      "state": {
        "thetaRotation": 45,
        "equationView": "Rotated x′,y′ equation"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare coordinates of one point",
      "narration": "Click a point on the ellipse to show projections onto both coordinate systems. The same physical point receives one coordinate pair in the black frame and a different pair in the orange rotated frame.",
      "state": {
        "thetaRotation": 45,
        "equationView": "Rotated x′,y′ equation"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A rotated conic is not a different curve; it is the same curve described in a better-aligned coordinate system, which is why the xy term can disappear after rotation.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use a clean white background with no grid or tick marks. Draw the fixed x and y axes as thin black double-headed arrows crossing at the center. Draw the rotated axes in bright orange, also double-headed, sharing the same origin; at the default angle the x′-axis should run diagonally from lower left to upper right and the y′-axis from upper left to lower right. The ellipse should be a dark blue outline centered at the origin, elongated along x′ and shorter along y′. Keep the black axes stationary under the slider while the orange axes, θ arc, θ label, and ellipse rotate together. Equation displays may be placed beside or below the diagram; in the original view highlight the xy cross term, and in the rotated view emphasize that only x′² and y′² terms remain. When a point is clicked, show two differently colored sets of projection lines, one matching the black axes and one matching the orange axes, with compact coordinate readouts near the point."
}

